<description>
Connect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.

Input structure
- The dominant color is the background.
- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.
- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.

Rule
- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.
- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.
- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.

Key insight
- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "non-background, non-teal colors with multiple 4-connected components",
  "path": "from each component to the next same-colored component in row-major order, take a shortest clear one-bend horizontal/vertical path through background or same color; teal wall segments and other colors block the path",
  "dst": "next same-colored component in the ordered chain",
  "trajectory": "paint every background pixel on each connector path with the source color",
  "transform": "keep teal wall segments, single-component colors, and all original foreground pixels unchanged",
  "multi_step": "process each color's components in row-major order; when both bends are clear, preserve the previous travel axis, otherwise use the longer-axis-first clear bend"
}
</annotation>
</reference_puzzle>

